    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jose Caballero** (66.13) and **Josh Naylor** (66.07) anchor our offensive effort with the highest efficiency scores. **Jose Caballero** remains a high-priority start despite a dynamic platoon disadvantage because he benefits from a park-factor boost of 16% and a favorable matchup against Brandon Young (xERA 4.78). Meanwhile, **Josh Naylor** capitalizes on a high-value #3 order spot against Peter Lambert; his projections are bolstered by a 5% platoon edge, making him our safest bet for RBI production. **Corbin Carroll** (59.75) sits right on the cusp of the \"Strong\" threshold; while Nathan Eovaldi presents a difficult assignment, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s elite sprint speed and favorable #2 lineup spot provide enough base-stealing potential to warrant a start despite the sub-par SP matchup.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today was benching **Dalton Rushing** (39.48), who finished just shy of the 40.0 floor. While **Dalton Rushing** offers stability, the optimizer favored **Gabriel Moreno** (41.23) for the C1 slot due to a slightly more favorable run-creation environment, even though both catchers are hampered by the \"Lineup Pending\" status and the risk of late-afternoon scratching. Elsewhere, **Cole Young** (42.11) occupies a tenuous spot in the lineup; although his score qualifies for a start, his placement in the #9 hole against Peter Lambert severely limits his counting stat ceiling. We are keeping **Travis Bazzana** (35.64) on the bench due to a poor platoon matchup against Suter, while **James Wood** remains sidelined as his team is off the board today. Monitor the late-breaking lineups for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Cole Young**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Corbin Carroll** closely, as the \"Lineup Pending\" designation introduces the highest level of variance for our current configuration.",
